 "P2": {
  "T:paper": {
   "x": 497,
   "y": 66.5,
   "w": 396.5,
   "h": 1513,
   "html": "<div class=\"paper\" style=\"text-wrap: wrap;\">\n<p class=\"pt pt1\" style=\"line-height: 1;\"><span style=\"font-size: 21px; letter-spacing: 0.02em;\"></span><span style=\"font-size: 25px;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0.02em;\">Superimposing </span><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0.42px;\">....</span></span><span style=\"font-size: 21px; letter-spacing: 0.42px;\"></span></p><p class=\"pt pt1\" style=\"line-height: 1;\"><span style=\"font-size: 21px;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0.02em;\">Square, Triangle and Circle</span></span><span style=\"font-size: 16px; letter-spacing: 0.02em;\">&nbsp;</span></p><p class=\"pt pt1\" style=\"line-height: 1;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px; letter-spacing: 0.28px;\">Constructive Geometry &amp;&nbsp;</span><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0.02em; font-size: 14px;\">The Integration of \u221a2 and \u221a3&nbsp;</span></p><p class=\"pt pt1\" style=\"line-height: 1;\"><br></p><p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-wrap: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 19px;\">The Marusankakushikaku</span>....Is a mysterious Japanese scroll painted by the beloved master artist &amp; monk, Sengai Gibo and is dated to&nbsp;<span style=\"text-indent: 2em;\">around 1824. It is often referred to as Japan's First Zen Temple. The inscription on the left of the scroll is</span><span style=\"text-indent: 30px;\">&nbsp;the name of Sengai's home temple of Shofuku-ji.&nbsp; In Zen teachings the diagram of these 3 primordial shapes can be interpreted as the underlying building blocks of the \"Universe\".&nbsp;</span></p><p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-wrap: wrap;\"><span style=\"text-indent: 2em;\">Both, as flat 2-dimensional shapes and 3-dimensional spatial&nbsp;</span><span style=\"text-indent: 2em;\">volumes they have historically been used as symbolic and contemplative devices to inspire the mind. The balanced integration of the qualities associated with each symbol is believed to raise one's awareness and&nbsp; induce realizations of the interconnective order of the cosmos.</span></p>\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-wrap: wrap;\"><span style=\"text-indent: 2em;\">The <u>SQUARE</u> is symbolic of the earth and the world of manifestation. The <u>TRIANGLE</u>, as it is in the Pythagorean mystery schools, is symbolic of fire and the world of spirit. T</span>he \"<u>CIRCLE</u>\" is perhaps&nbsp; in a category of it's own and often referred to as the \"ENSO\". It is more complex and enigmatic than the others often given contrasting meanings such as 'empty' yet 'full', 'expanding' yet 'limiting', and 'eternal' yet 'cyclical'.</p><p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-wrap: wrap;\"><span style=\"text-indent: 2em;\">The \"LAYING OUT OF THE CIRCLE\" is often regarded as the first act of temple building. Keith Critchlow in his book \"Time Stands Still\", explains that the word 'temple' has its roots in the ancient Greek Temnos, which means \"to cut off\" or \"separate\". In Latin.... \"templum\" meaning \"Space\", has survived in modern languages as the word 'template', similar to the word \"tempus\" and confers the adjacent meaning of...\"Time\" <span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">(Critchlow, p22)</span>. In the earliest accounts of temple construction, found in the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span style=\"text-indent: 2em;\">Hindu manuscript the Manasara Shilpa Shastra, <u>the initial act of temple construction is to establish a central point or axis around which a circle is drawn</u>. This circumscription defines a sacred microcosm within which a transcendent relationship can be established bringing</span>&nbsp;one into attunement with the correct order and harmony of the cosmos.&nbsp;</p><p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-wrap: wrap;\">With respect to these 3 shapes it is intriguing to consider their combined meaning when aligned to each other and geometrically juxtaposed. Using the circle as the \"limiting element\" with a radius of&nbsp; one, or unity, we find that the edge length of the square within the circle is the square root of two (\u221a2), and that the edge length of the triangle within the circle is the square root of three (\u221a3).&nbsp; <u>It turns out that the edge length of the square, \u221a2,&nbsp; is precisely the height of a TETRAHEDRON whose base is the same dimensions as the triangle</u>&nbsp; originally circumscribed within the circle <span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">(Fig. 3)</span>.</p><p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><span style=\"color: rgb(240, 228, 212); font-size: 14px; font-weight: 700; text-align: left;\">Works Cited</span></p>\n<p class=\"wc\" style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Addiss, Stephen. The Art of Zen<br>New York: Harry N. Abrams, Inc., 1989</p>\n<p class=\"wc\" style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Critchlow, Keith. Time Stands Still<br>New York: St. Martin's Press, 1982</p>\n<p class=\"wc\" style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Kapraff, Jay. Connections</p><p class=\"wc\" style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-indent: 0px;\">(P</span><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.36px; text-indent: 0px;\">ublished in the 1994 International Conference on Space Structures-</span><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.36px; text-indent: 0px;\">4/5/94</span><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-size: 12px; text-indent: 0px; letter-spacing: 0.02em;\">)</span></p>\n</div>\n",
   "t": 1794323162423
  },
  "I:images/P2/P2-01_the-symbols-of-the-1st-temple.jpg": {
