  "T:excerpt": {
   "x": 43.5,
   "y": 1046.5,
   "w": 440,
   "h": 600,
   "html": "<div class=\"paper excerpt\">\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">It is intriguing to consider the meaning emerging from the integration of these shapes in terms of both their geometric and mathematical relationships. For if we superimpose the three, using the circle as the limiting shape, we discover a very interesting set of relationships (Fig. 2). Given the radius of the circle as unity or one, we find that the edge length of the square within the circle is the square root of two, and that the edge length of the triangle within the circle is the square root of three. The significance of these relationships is that the edge length of the square is precisely the height of a tetrahedron whose base is the same triangle originally circumscribed by the circle (Fig. 3).</p>\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><br></p>\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><br></p>\n</div>\n",
   "hide": true,
   "t": 1794062220265
  },
