  "NT:mw9c7x9py6n1": {
   "new": "text",
   "x": 494.5,
   "y": 754,
   "w": 392,
   "h": 560,
   "html": "<h3 class=\"ph\" style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 0.7; text-wrap: wrap;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: var(--body); font-size: 16px;\">In the photo above you can see Bucky's exploration with \"parallel ruled lines\" of warped planes over the exterior surfaces.&nbsp; Because of my experience building&nbsp; kites with internal bracing I decided to do the opposite, to explore the INTERIOR of the Tetra-helix instead. I created the first models using wooden dowels and plastic tubes with colored threads of Red and Blue string crossing through the internal centroids of each tetrahedron from the tetrahedral edges. This created 2 very beautiful hyperbolic-paraboloids that interpenetrated each other, and which in turn created an unexpected \"Center Core Channel\".&nbsp; Although the core looks circular when viewed down the axis of the tetrahelix, at any 90\u00ba cross section the shape proves to actually be two opposite arcs creating an almond shaped center area similar to the \"Vesica Pisces\", a very important and meaningful symbol in Christianity and other spiritual belief systems. The Vesica Pisces is a fundamental shape in Sacred Geometry.&nbsp;</span><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: var(--body); font-size: 16px;\">I began to think that Tetra-Wave was more a model of an eletromagnetic wave or some kind of healing energy form.&nbsp; This is just one of the many correlations that began to emerge.</span></h3>",
   "t": 1794341063353
  },
  "T:MODELS": {
   "x": 302,
   "y": -3.5,
   "w": 377,
   "h": 82,
   "html": "<span style=\"font-size: 32px; color: rgb(245, 202, 151); font-family: var(--display);\">Kites to Tetra-Helices</span>",
   "t": 1794333262356
  },
  "NT:mwaocjg9im2k": {
   "new": "text",
   "x": 495.5,
   "y": 76,
   "w": 392,
   "h": 329,
   "html": "<h3 class=\"ph\" style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 0.7; text-wrap: wrap;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-size: 16px; font-family: var(--body);\">The first time I saw a Tetrahedron was in a biography entitled \"Genius At Work\" about Alexander Graham Bell.&nbsp; Of his many inventions, from the Iron Lung to aircraft wings for the Navy, I loved the large TETRAHEDRON KITES built of metal tubes, some of which were large enough with sufficient force to&nbsp; lift people and animals right off the ground...defeating gravity!&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></h3><h3 class=\"ph\" style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 0.7; text-wrap: wrap;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: var(--body); font-size: 16px;\">Inspired by his tetrahedral kites I started building my own, exploring ways of making them stronger and lighter using internal bracing from opposite outer edges following the natural vectors of edge sub-divisions, what I later learned was called \"cell-frequency\", and even more recently a mathematical function called \"Fractals\".</span></h3>",
   "t": 1794341010733
  },
